 a one-point race. Markets and the weight of expert ratings tilt slightly toward Wittman, so the forecast lands just on the Republican side of even with wide uncertainty.", "watch": "Any additional independent polling or outside spending in the final weeks could clarify whether Taylor's surge is real or whether Wittman's financial edge reasserts itself.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Rob Wittman (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Virginia's 1st District has emerged as a genuine battleground, with incumbent Republican Rob Wittman defending a seat against Democratic challenger Shannon Taylor in a district that leaned Republican by three points at the last presidential election. The race has drawn national attention because House control could hinge on it, and expert raters are split between Lean R and Tossup ratings.\n\nTaylor has kept the race close despite a significant financial disadvantage, and the one recent poll of the race showed the candidates separated by a single point. Prediction markets give Taylor roughly a four-in-ten chance of winning. The combination of Wittman's incumbency and fundraising edge against a favorable midterm climate for Democrats leaves this race essentially even, with a slight Republican tilt.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4506, "margin": -0.72}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4359, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4341, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4371, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3778, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.3736, "margin": -1.5}]}